1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://kvindebiografiskleksikon.lex.dk/Johanne_Luise_Heiberg
2 CONT Kort uddrag:
2 CONT Johanne Luise Heiberg, født 22. november 1812, København, død 21. december 1890, København.
2 CONT Arbejdsliv Skuespiller, forfatter, danser og instruktør
2 CONT Familie
2 CONT Forældre: vintapper, traktør Christian Heinrich Joseph Pätges (1777-1834) og Henriette Hartwig (ca. 1780-1861).
2 CONT Gift 31. juli 1831 med forfatter Johan Ludvig H., født 14. december 1791 i København, død 25. august 1860 på Bonderup, Store Tåstrup sogn, s. af forfatter Peter Andreas H. og Thomasine Buntzen (forfatter Thomasine Gyllembourg).
2 CONT Adoptivbørn: Sarah Henriette (1853), Lelia Adela (1855), Anna Louise (1857).
2 CONT *
2 CONT http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johanne_Luise_Heiberg
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sa.dk/ao-soegesider/da/billedviser?bsid=156133#156133,26144131 Petri Tyske   kirke 1812
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sa.dk/ao-soegesider/da/billedviser?bsid=379965#379965,73898179  Trinitatis   1828 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sa.dk/ao-soegesider/da/billedviser?bsid=205808#205808,39062478  Slangerup 1831
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sa.dk/ao-soegesider/da/billedviser?bsid=151464#151464,25164829 Hof og Slotskirken  1890
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.sa.dk/ao-soegesider/da/billedviser?epid=19931950#451610,80960512 Dødsattest
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gravsted.dk/person.php?navn=johanneluiseheiberg
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanne Luise Heiberg (née Pätges) (22 November 1812 – 21 December 1890) was one of the greatest Danish actresses of the 19th century. She is most famous for her work at the Royal Theatre in Copenhagen, where she achieved great su
2 CONC ccess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like Hans Christian Andersen she originally belonged to a poor milieu. She was the daughter of a stallholder and innkeeper and of his Jewish wife, very early she showed artistic gifts and entered the ballet school 1820. By the hel
2 CONC p of patrons she was promoted to the rank of an actress and made a successful debut 1827. From then she for more than thirty years appeared the absolutely leading actress of Danish theatre.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1831 she married the famous and much older critic and dramatist Johan Ludvig Heiberg, a marriage that raised her position even further and just made her known as “Mrs. Heiberg”. “The Heibergs” became a Copenhagen concept and th
2 CONC eir home was a cultural centre. In return the dual position of the Heibergs as the leading lady of the theatre married to its main playwright also aroused much jealousy and accusations of favouring. Her great popularity within the
2 CONC  public did not prevent her of getting opponents and her sometimes aggressive and arrogant one-sidedness (revealed in her autobiography) must be blamed for some of this. Her husband’s time as the director of the Royal Theatre 1849
2 CONC -56 ended in an open conflict with her colleagues and for a short period she even left the theatre. The death of her husband 1860 and her age caused her retirement as an actress 1864 — still at her height — though she worked as a 
2 CONC stage director until 1874.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Heiberg played about 275 roles. Her exotic beauty combined with culture and elegance secured her position though she was not the only talented Danish actress of her age. Among her roles must be mentioned some Shakespeare char
2 CONC acters (Viola in Twelfth Night) and especially in French comedies and dramas. In Danish dramas she shined in Holberg’s and Oehlenschlägers plays and especially in her husband’s dramas, her performance in Elves' Hill (Danish: Elver
2 CONC høi) was a classic. Her strength was intelligence, controlled passion and wit but she did not show the same talent for the tragedy. To a whole generation of Danish dramatists she was the muse and especially she inspired Henrik Her
2 CONC tz to write many of his main female roles for her. She also wrote some few (not especially important) vaudeville acts herself; the most popular is En Søndag paa Amager (“A Sunday at Amager”, 1845).
2 CONT
2 CONT Soren Kierkegaard wrote a tribute to her in 1847, The Crisis and a Crisis in the Life of an Actress. Her autobiography, Et Liv gjenoplevet i Erindringen ("A Life Relived in Memory", ed. 1891-92) is a major literary work from the D
2 CONC anish Golden Age. It has often been criticised for its subjective descriptions but is still regarded a pioneering work because of its interest of the process of acting. Though she was closely connected to the romantic tradition Mr
2 CONC s. Heiberg is still regarded a key figure of Danish drama, like for instance Sarah Siddons in England. Beyond any doubt she contributed to the rise of the public social and moral opinion of Danish actors as play-actors to artists 
2 CONC and cultural personalities. Her image is currently featured on the front of the Danish 200-krone banknote.[1][2] The drama Rain Snakes (Från regnormarnas liv, 1981) by the Swedish author Per-Olov Enquist fictitiously deals with th
2 CONC e relationship between Mrs. Heiberg and Hans Christian Andersen.
2 CONT
2 CONT
